{
 "id": "2023-kang-perceived",
 "name": "Perceived Walkability: Street View Pairs (Jeonju)",
 "description": "Method replication: same task, scales and design as the paper; the images are not the paper's stimuli (66 new Google Street View views of Jeonju, 2015–2018; the paper used Kakao street views).\n\nKang, Kim, Park & Lee (2023, ISPRS IJGI) compared perceived and physical walkability across Jeonju, a medium-sized Korean city. On their \"Street view image based walkability survey\" site, participants saw two street-view images side by side and answered \"Which one do you think is better for walking?\", choosing the left image, the right image or \"=\" (equal). 52 respondents gave 127,317 comparisons (December 2021 to March 2022), every image compared at least six times; the \"=\" answers (6,422) were left out of model training. A global-patch Siamese/RankNet model then predicted perceived walkability for all 196,624 images and was compared with a physical walkability index from semantic segmentation and GIS data.\n\nThis template keeps the question, the side-by-side layout and the three answers. Results ranks the images with TrueSkill; the \"=\" answers are stored as ties.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) images: the paper crawled Kakao street views at 30 m intervals in four directions (front, right, back, left) and stratified the survey set by road type and land use; here 66 Google Street View views from a grid over Jeonju, each facing one of the four directions at random, with a few views of bare walls or slopes removed, paired at random instead of the K-disjoint / K-chaining pair design; (2) 30 pairs per participant (the original respondents each answered thousands); (3) one optional question on familiarity with Jeonju added.\n\nStreet images: Google Street View (© Google).",
 "author": "Kang, Kim, Park & Lee",
